## 1. PLAN

### 1.1 Context and constraints

A (DOCX, PDF) pair describes the same paper. Pipeline:

1. **Extract** math items + metadata from DOCX and from the tagged PDF (PDF items carry
   x/y/bbox used later to crop images).
2. **Map** each DOCX math item to its PDF counterpart (this task).
3. **Compare** mapped items image-based (crops via PDF coords). **Unchanged.**

The legacy mapping was brittle and overfit: text-based, tuned per file, and its own output
*is* the reference ground truth available to us. Constraints therefore:

* deterministic, free, offline only (no ML training, no paid API);
* no per-file special cases;
* must not regress pairs that already work;
* comparison logic must not be modified.

### 1.2 Design in one paragraph

Canonicalize both sides' math strings into one comparable alphabet (Step A), turn them into
cheap set/multiset features (Step B), score every candidate pair with availability-aware
weighted components (Step C.1-C.2), cut the item sequences into coarse regions that only
compare locally (Step D), solve each region with an **order-preserving DP** (Step C.3, this
round) so repeated expressions cannot cross-map, and expose the whole thing as diagnostics
plus additive payload keys (Step E). Embeddings exist behind a flag (Step F) but are off.

### 1.3 Steps A-F (what each step actually does)

**Step A - canonicalization** (`math_canonicalize.py`, public API `canonicalize(math_str) -> str`)

* Unicode NFKC + dash/quote/space folding (NBSP, thin spaces, U+2212 math minus -> `-`).
* Strip purely visual tokens: `\left \right \, \! \; \: \quad \qquad \displaystyle
  \textstyle \limits \nolimits`, `\big`..`\Bigg` variants, `~`, redundant brace nesting.
* Conservative macro unify: `\dfrac`/`\tfrac`/`\cfrac` -> `\frac`, `\cdotp` -> `\cdot`,
  font wrappers (`\mathrm`/`\mathit`/`\mathbf`/`\text`) unwrapped because they are styling,
  not semantics. Semantically risky merges are deliberately NOT done.
* LaTeX structure rewrite: `array`/`matrix`/`cases` environments -> bracket form,
  `\sum\limits_{a}^{b}` -> `sum(a..b)`, `\frac`/script normalisation, `\sqrt[n]{}` layout.
* Spoken math (`_canonicalize_spoken`, gated by `looks_like_spoken_math`) maps PDF-side
  spoken text ("one half x squared") into the same alphabet as the LaTeX side, so both
  media land in one comparable space.
* `is_placeholder_math_text()` flags `inline-eq-IEqNN`-style placeholder ids that Word/
  Springer emit, so callers can treat such items as content-less.
* Optional backends probed at import time, never required: `pylatexenc` (token walk),
  `sympy` (`sympify`/`srepr` key), `latex2sympy2`, LaTeXML via CLI.
  `optional_backend_status()` reports which are live; falling back to the raw string logs a
  warning.
* Deterministic, no I/O beyond the optional backend probe. `CANONICALIZATION_VERSION = "1.0"`
  is stamped into the engine summary so any change here is visible in the payload.

**Step B - features** (`math_mapping.py`, `MathItemFeatures`)

Per item, from canonical text: `tokens` (Counter multiset) + `token_set`, `symbols`
(single-char identifiers/operators), `operators` (counts), `label` (visible number such as
`(12)`), `section_key` (block anchor), prose `context_before`/`context_after`, `family`
(`inline`/`display`), `index`/`order`. Helpers: `jaccard_multiset`, `symbol_overlap`,
`text_similarity` (difflib ratio on canonical strings). All pure and deterministic.

**Step C - scoring + assignment**

C.1/C.2 components, each `float | None` where `None` means *unavailable* (no evidence on
either side) -> the component is dropped and the remaining weights are renormalised:

| component | meaning |
|---|---|
| `text_similarity` | difflib ratio of canonical strings |
| `token_jaccard` | multiset Jaccard of tokens |
| `symbol_overlap` | Jaccard of symbol sets |
| `label` | visible number: 1.0 match, 0.5 one-sided, 0.0 conflict (+ conflict flag) |
| `context` | difflib ratio of de-placeholdered prose windows around the item |
| `section` | block key agreement: 1.0 same key, 0.6 same namespace prefix, else 0.0 |
| `position` | `1 - |relative index difference|` (weak diagonal prior) |
| `visual` | crop/image similarity (weight 0.0, i.e. effectively off) |
| `embedding` | sentence-transformer cosine (Step F, off by default) |

C.3 **assignment**: per Step D region, order-preserving DP by default
(`solve_monotone_assignment`, Needleman-Wunsch style, gap cost 0, ties prefer a match, any
pair below `confidence_threshold` is dropped). The fallback used when
`use_monotone_assignment=False` is the older `_monotonicize(solve_assignment(numeric), ...)`
pair: scipy's `linear_sum_assignment` (with a greedy fallback when scipy is missing) followed
by a single-swap repair whose margin is `monotonic_swap_margin`. Acceptance = score >=
`confidence_threshold` (0.40) minus `label_conflict_penalty` (0.10) when both labels exist and
differ; items the solver leaves unpaired stay unmapped (they are not force-paired).

**Step D - region decomposition** (`sequence_regions`)

`difflib.SequenceMatcher` over (section key, family, label) sequences gives `anchor`,
`replace`, `insert`, `delete` regions; items only compete inside one region. This is the
anti-cascade mechanism: a mis-extracted item cannot drag the whole document off diagonal,
and skipped spans surface as genuine unmapped items instead of being force-paired.

**Step E - output** (`MappingResult.as_dict`, `write_diagnostics`)

Pairs with per-component breakdown, regions, summary (counts, families, acceptance stats,
canonicalization version/backends), config echo, score-matrix size cap, plus
`unmapped_docx`/`unmapped_pdf` with reasons. `write_diagnostics(output_dir, stem=...)`
emits per-run CSVs (`mapping_candidates.csv`, `mapping_pairs.csv`, ...) and JSON.

**Step F - optional embedding** (`enable_embedding_feature=False` by default)

`sentence-transformers` (`all-MiniLM-L6-v2` default) is imported lazily, only when the flag
and a non-zero weight are both set. It is a free/local model, but it is deliberately off so
the shipped path stays dependency-light and fully deterministic.


### 1.4 Weights and thresholds (final values)

| key | env var | default | why this value |
|---|---|---|---|
| `weights.text_similarity` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_WEIGHT_TEXT` | 0.28 | best single signal when both sides have real math text |
| `weights.token_jaccard` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_WEIGHT_TOKENS` | 0.14 | catches re-ordered LaTeX spellings |
| `weights.symbol_overlap` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_WEIGHT_SYMBOLS` | 0.14 | robust to structural noise |
| `weights.label` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_WEIGHT_LABEL` | 0.15 | equation numbers are near-unique anchors |
| `weights.context` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_WEIGHT_CONTEXT` | 0.20 | strongest signal on placeholder-only PDFs |
| `weights.section` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_WEIGHT_SECTION` | 0.08 | weak tie-breaker, cheap |
| `weights.position` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_WEIGHT_POSITION` | 0.08 | small on purpose: never let it dominate |
| `weights.visual` / `weights.embedding` | `..._WEIGHT_VISUAL` / `..._WEIGHT_EMBEDDING` | 0.0 | available but off |
| `confidence_threshold` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD` | 0.40 | accept floor; also the DP floor |
| `rescue_threshold` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_RESCUE_THRESHOLD` | 0.60 | secondary acceptance for visual rescue |
| `visual_top_k` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_VISUAL_TOP_K` | 5 | candidates considered for visual rescue |
| `label_conflict_penalty` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_LABEL_CONFLICT_PENALTY` | 0.10 | two different visible numbers are strong evidence |
| `enable_sequence_alignment` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_ENABLE_SEQUENCE_ALIGNMENT` | true | Step D regions on/off |
| `enable_embedding_feature` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_ENABLE_EMBEDDING` | false | Step F opt-in |
| `embedding_model_name` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_EMBEDDING_MODEL` | `all-MiniLM-L6-v2` | only used when Step F enabled |
| `monotonic_swap_margin` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_MONOTONIC_SWAP_MARGIN` | 0.0 | fallback (Hungarian) path only |
| `pdf_reading_order` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_PDF_READING_ORDER` | **false** | **new**: tag order is the default (see §1.6) |
| `use_monotone_assignment` | `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_MONOTONE_ASSIGNMENT` | **true** | **new**: order-preserving DP (see §1.7) |

Provenance: weights came from a bounded grid search over the pair set
(`temp/grid2.py`); they are *not* re-tuned per document - per-pair tuning was explicitly
rejected as overfitting (§3.6). All keys are echoed in the payload under
`mapping_engine.config`, so any run can be reproduced from its output.

### 1.5 Decision log (what was chosen, what was rejected, why)

| decision | chosen | rejected | why |
|---|---|---|---|
| math alphabet | canonical string + tokens + symbols (Step A/B) | raw LaTeX string equality; full LaTeXML MathML requirement | raw equality fails on visual macros; LaTeXML is optional in this env, so it must never be required |
| scores | availability-aware weighted components, renormalised | hard feature vector with fixed weights | unavailable components (e.g. no math text on the PDF side) would otherwise push every score below the floor |
| case pairing | region-local assignment (Step D + C.3) | global one-shot assignment over all items | global assignment cascades: one bad item shifts everything after it |
| matching criterion | order preserving by default | order-agnostic (Hungarian) | reference alignments are (nearly) monotone; crossing matches were the main systematic error |
| acceptance | threshold + label-conflict penalty | threshold only | different visible numbers on the two sides mean "not the same item" |
| ground truth | legacy alignment captured as fixtures, explicitly marked `PENDING_REVIEW` | treating the legacy output as verified truth | the legacy mapping is the thing we are fixing, so it must be reviewable, not gospel |

### 1.6 Ordering decision (this round): PDF tag order is the default

`align_items(..., use_pdf_reading_order=None)` now defaults to the PDF **tag** order
(`config.pdf_reading_order = False`); the geometric sort (top-to-bottom, left-to-right, with
line merging at `READING_ORDER_LINE_HEIGHT = 12.0`) is opt-in per config/env.

Evidence:

* `temp/sweep_reading_order.py` mixes the order used for DOCX and PDF sides and reports the
  harness mean F1. Tag order on both sides wins; geometric PDF order costs several points
  because floated/column content and figure-embedded equations are emitted late, which makes
  the PDF sequence non-monotone w.r.t. the DOCX sequence.
* `temp/diag_order.py` measures, per pair, how many reference pairs are order-violating under
  each order. Under tag order the references are ~99% monotone; under geometric order there
  are large inversion clusters on the pairs that used to fail.
* Word-exported tagged PDFs are written in authoring order, i.e. the same logical order as
  the DOCX body, so tag order is the *document* order and geometric order is a heuristic on
  top of an already-correct sequence.

The geometric path is kept (not deleted) because it protects against PDFs whose tag order is
scrambled by a different producer; `MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_PDF_READING_ORDER=1` restores it.


### 1.7 Assignment decision (this round): monotone DP inside regions

`solve_monotone_assignment(scores, accept_floor)` is a Needleman-Wunsch style DP over the
region's score matrix: `dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1], dp[i-1][j-1] + score[i][j])`,
with ties resolved in favour of taking the match, and matched pairs below `accept_floor`
removed afterwards. Unmatched rows/columns become unmapped items (`pending_rows`/`pending_cols`
start as the full region ranges).

Evidence / rationale:

* The reference alignments are monotone (see §1.6), so an unconstrained solver is free to
  produce crossings the reference never has; repeated identical expressions (`x`, `1`, `0`,
  inline single symbols) are exactly where Hungarian chose the wrong copy and produced
  *swaps* - the dominant residual error class (§3.5).
* Region-local monotonicity is a much weaker constraint than global monotonicity: Step D
  already cuts the document into anchors/replacements, so a genuinely reordered block only
  loses the freedom to permute *inside* that block.
* Hungarian (`solve_assignment`, scipy `linear_sum_assignment` + `_monotonicize` swap repair)
  is retained as the fallback (`MATHCOMPARE_MAPPING_MONOTONE_ASSIGNMENT=0`), so the previous
  behaviour can be reproduced exactly for A/B testing and nothing is deleted.

