### 3.2 What a fixture means

`tests/fixtures/mapping_pairs/<paper_id>.json`, one per paper:

| field | meaning |
|---|---|
| `status` | `PENDING_REVIEW` = generated from current engine output, **human review pending**; `VERIFIED` = a human confirmed the alignment. All 16 are `PENDING_REVIEW` today |
| `reference` / `reference_kind` | `sidecar` = legacy pipeline output from `watch_output/.../raw_vs_tagged_alignment.json` (the only ground truth that exists for that pair); `self_reference` = no legacy sidecar exists, so the fixture records current engine output as a stand-in |
| `expected_pairs` | the reference `(docx_order, pdf_order)` pairs; F1 is computed against exactly this set |
| `target_f1` | 0.95 = the "correct mapping" goal used for the pass/fail summary |
| `min_f1` | **regression floor**: for `PENDING_REVIEW` fixtures it is `observed F1 - 0.05`; for `VERIFIED` fixtures it is 0.0 (a verified pair must simply not crash). The pytest gate fails only on a drop below the floor |
| `max_unmapped_docx` / `max_unmapped_pdf` | observed unmapped count + 2 slack |
| `observed` | full outcome echo (counts, precision/recall/F1, min accepted score, order used) |
| `notes` | provenance/review note |

F1 convention: `predicted` pairs vs `expected` pairs, `2PR/(P+R)`, with the empty-vs-empty
case defined as 1.0 (a pair whose PDF has no math items at all is not a recall failure).

Regression semantics: a pair counted "passing target" only when `F1 >= 0.95`; the gate that
**blocks** a commit is `F1 >= min_f1` plus the unmapped bounds. Re-baselining
(`--write-fixtures`) re-records observations but never upgrades `status` - only a human
editing the fixture to `VERIFIED` does that.

